  title={Adaptive Routing in MANETs using Hydro-AI: A Hybrid Deep Reinforcement Learning-based Approach}, 
  year={2025},
  volume={},
  number={},
  pages={1047-1055},
  abstract={The dynamic topologies, decentralized architecture, and lack of stable infrastructure that define Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANETs) make them efficient routing a fundamental challenge. Because of their reactive route discovery and frequent route breakdowns, traditional routing protocols like AODV and DSR perform poorly in high-mobility situations mechanisms. In this study, a novel hybrid routing framework that combines LSTM (Deep Learning) and Reinforcement Learning (DQN) to enable intelligent, adaptive, and proactive routing decisions in MANETs. The LSTM network, or Long Short-Term Memory, is used to forecast node mobility and link stability based on historical movement and signal data, while a Deep Q-Network (DQN) learns optimal next-hop routing policies through continuous interaction with the network environment. The routing agent dynamically incorporates link predictions into its state space, enabling it to avoid unstable paths before they break. Simulation results in NS-3 demonstrate that the proposed method significantly outperforms traditional protocols in regard to energy consumption, route stability, end-to-end latency and packet delivery ratio. The inclusion of profound and reinforcement learning presents a scalable and robust solution for routing Vehicle networks are examples of extremely dynamic and heterogeneous MANET environments and UAV-assisted communication systems.},
  keywords={Sensor systems;Aerospace and electronic systems;Feeds;Radio broadcasting;Frequency modulation;Feedback;Filtering;Circuits;Filters;MIMICs;Mobile Ad Hoc Networks;Routing protocols;Route discovery;Deep Learning and Reinforcement Learning},
